{
 "intro": "Very red WISE sources (W1\u2212W2 > 1.5) are either cold brown dwarfs (methane hides them at 3.4 \u00b5m) or distant dusty active galaxies. WISE colours alone can't tell them apart. Proper motion can: brown dwarfs are close, so they move. We read each source's SPHEREx 0.75\u20135 \u00b5m spectrum and asked Wanderer's classifier what it is. The methane test uses the spectrum alone; the final label also uses the catalogue motion. Our first classifier failed this test (it called 6 of 8 galaxies brown dwarfs), which is how we found and fixed its flaw.",
 "method": "Targets: CatWISE2020, 8 fields \u00d7 6\u00b0 radius at high galactic latitude, 8 < W2 < 14, clean photometry. 'Moving' = proper motion > 0.15\u2033/yr at > 5\u03c3; 'not moving' = < 2\u03c3. Up to 180 SPHEREx QR2 frames per source, 3\u2032 cut-outs. Colours self-calibrated on field stars in each cut-out. Each source is measured at the brightest 4.6 \u00b5m pixel within 1 px of its predicted position, so very faint ones (e.g. the single 'cold brown dwarf') can change label when you click a neighbouring pixel: treat them as leads.",
 "summary": {
  "lines": [
   "<b>8</b> moving (nearby, brown-dwarf-like): 4\u00d7 Not detected, 1\u00d7 Cold brown dwarf, 1\u00d7 Unclear (noisy colours), 1\u00d7 Normal star, 1\u00d7 Too bright (saturated)",
   "<b>30</b> not moving (distant, galaxy-like): 29\u00d7 Dusty active galaxy (likely), 1\u00d7 Unclear (noisy colours)",
   "Brown-dwarf label on non-moving sources (false alarms): <b>0/30</b>",
   "Brown-dwarf label on moving sources: <b>1/8</b>",
   "Methane trough (spectrum only, no motion used) on non-moving sources: <b>0/26</b> \u00b7 Epsilon Indi B for comparison: 74%",
   "<b>4/8</b> 'moving' catalogue sources are not seen by SPHEREx at all, though they should be bright enough: probably spurious CatWISE entries"
  ]
 },
